IMPORT prelude

-- District Grand Master of the Eastern Archipelago Ordinance 1939 (Singapore) -- who the Corporation is and how its authority is
-- proved (ss 2, 7), what it may hold and the trusts it holds it on (ss 3, 4), what a purchaser or anyone dealing with it may rely on
-- (ss 3(2), 5), how its deeds are executed (s 6), and the Government's rights (s 8).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/DGMEAO1939.txt. The deposit says the revised edition
-- "incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021"; the only
-- amendment annotated in the body is [7/97] on s 8 (the Legislative History lists Act 7 of 1997, Statutes (Miscellaneous
-- Amendments) Act 1997, in force 1 October 1997). The deposit's metadata records it as the current version as at 1 October 2026.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0232 in subjects/sg/requirements.jsonl: Tier 4 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by
-- everyday-life relevance. The requirement asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked
-- a sharper question yet. The people it decides anything for are a Lodge or trustee handing Masonic property to the Corporation, and
-- a buyer, mortgagee, tenant or other person dealing with the Corporation. The whole Ordinance (8 sections) is in scope.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: s 1 (short title); s 2(2) (the seal may be changed) beyond a comment; the general law the Ordinance displaces or
-- relies on -- s 15 of the Trustees Act 1967 and s 48 of the Conveyancing and Law of Property Act 1886 were not read; the general
-- laws and regulations of the Craft to which s 4(2) subjects the Corporation's directions; what "the rights of the Government" in
-- s 8 are.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. NOT for public use. See NOTES.md.

§ `District Grand Master of the Eastern Archipelago Ordinance 1939`

§§ `2 and 7 -- the Corporation and proof of the office`

-- s 2(1): "Wilberforce Henry William Gubbins by patent of appointment from the Grand Master of the United Grand Lodge of Ancient Free
-- and Accepted Masons of England the District Grand Master of the Eastern Archipelago and his successors in office so authorised
-- and appointed shall be a body corporate" named "The District Grand Master of the Eastern Archipelago", with "perpetual
-- succession" and a corporate seal. (2): the seal "may from time to time be broken, changed, altered and made anew".
-- s 7: "A notification in the Gazette of the appointment of any person to exercise the office ... shall be conclusive evidence that
-- such person was duly authorised to exercise the said office."
--
-- The Corporation is a corporation sole in effect (an inference: the text names one office-holder and his successors). Who
-- appoints is the Grand Master of the United Grand Lodge of England, not any Singapore authority; Singapore law only records it.
DECLARE Appointer IS ONE OF
    `the Grand Master of the United Grand Lodge of England`
    `a Lodge in Singapore`
    `the Minister`
    `the previous District Grand Master`

GIVEN `appointed by` IS AN Appointer, `appointed by patent` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `appointed to the District Grand Mastership of the Eastern Archipelago` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is the Corporation's office-holder` IF
        `appointed by patent`
    AND `appointed to the District Grand Mastership of the Eastern Archipelago`
    AND CONSIDER `appointed by`
        WHEN `the Grand Master of the United Grand Lodge of England` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 7 makes a Gazette notification CONCLUSIVE; without one, authority may still be proved by other evidence (an inference: s 7
-- does not say a notification is the only proof).
GIVEN `appointment notified in the Gazette` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `authority to exercise the office is conclusively proved` IF `appointment notified in the Gazette`

§§ `3 and 4 -- property and the trusts it is held on`

-- s 3(1): the Corporation "may acquire, purchase, take, hold, and enjoy movable and immovable property of every description and
-- whether situate in the Colony or elsewhere soever and either by way of gift or for valuable consideration or partly in one way and
-- partly in the other, and may sell, convey, assign, surrender, yield up, mortgage, demise, re-assign, transfer or otherwise dispose
-- of" property vested in it "upon such terms as to the Corporation seem fit".
DECLARE Dealing IS ONE OF
    `take property by gift`
    `buy property`
    `take property partly by gift and partly for value`
    `hold property outside Singapore`
    `sell property`
    `mortgage property`
    `lease property`
    `surrender property`

GIVEN `the dealing` IS A Dealing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Corporation may make the dealing` IF
    CONSIDER `the dealing`
    WHEN `take property by gift` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `buy property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `take property partly by gift and partly for value` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `hold property outside Singapore` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `sell property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `mortgage property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `lease property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `surrender property` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 4(1): property "held by any person or body of persons in trust for the purposes of Free Masonry in general or of any Lodge or
-- body of Masons in particular" that is transferred to the Corporation "shall be held by the Corporation upon and for or according
-- to such trusts".
-- s 4(2): property in the Corporation's possession "which shall not be impressed with any trust express or implied shall be held by
-- the Corporation upon trust for the benefit of Free Masonry in general or for such charitable uses as are peculiar to the fraternity
-- of Free and Accepted Masons as the Corporation shall, subject to the general laws and regulations for the Government of the Craft,
-- from time to time direct or appoint".
--
-- Nothing the Corporation holds is held for itself beneficially: untrusted property is put on a Masonic trust by s 4(2). Property
-- impressed with some other, non-Masonic trust is not dealt with by s 4; that it stays on that trust is an inference.
DECLARE Provenance IS ONE OF
    `transferred by trustees holding it for a Lodge`
    `transferred by trustees holding it for Free Masonry in general`
    `given outright with no trust`
    `bought with no trust declared`
    `received impressed with a non-Masonic trust`

DECLARE Trust IS ONE OF
    `the trusts on which it was held before the transfer`
    `for Free Masonry in general or Masonic charitable uses as the Corporation directs`
    `the trust it is impressed with (inference)`

GIVEN `where it came from` IS A Provenance
GIVETH A Trust
DECIDE `the trust the property is held on` IS
    CONSIDER `where it came from`
    WHEN `transferred by trustees holding it for a Lodge` THEN `the trusts on which it was held before the transfer`
    WHEN `transferred by trustees holding it for Free Masonry in general` THEN `the trusts on which it was held before the transfer`
    WHEN `given outright with no trust` THEN `for Free Masonry in general or Masonic charitable uses as the Corporation directs`
    WHEN `bought with no trust declared` THEN `for Free Masonry in general or Masonic charitable uses as the Corporation directs`
    OTHERWISE `the trust it is impressed with (inference)`

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Corporation holds property beneficially for itself` IS FALSE

§§ `3(2) and 5 -- what a purchaser and anyone dealing with the Corporation may rely on`

-- s 3(2): the Corporation may "either alone or in conjunction with any other person or persons whether incorporated or not accept and
-- administer and either in the capacity of an operative or as a custodian trustee any trust express or implied and notwithstanding
-- anything to the contrary contained in section 15 of the Trustees Act 1967 whenever any immovable property is vested in the
-- Corporation as sole trustee upon trust for sale or with power of sale the Corporation shall have the right to give valid receipts
-- for the proceeds of sale or other capital money arising from such property".
--
-- The special receipt power needs all three: IMMOVABLE property, the Corporation as SOLE trustee, and a trust for sale or a power of
-- sale. Outside that, s 3(2) gives nothing and the general law decides (not encoded).
GIVEN `the property is immovable` IS A BOOLEAN, `the Corporation is sole trustee` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `held on trust for sale or with power of sale` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 3(2) makes the Corporation's receipt valid` IF
    `the property is immovable` AND `the Corporation is sole trustee` AND `held on trust for sale or with power of sale`

DECLARE TrusteeRole IS ONE OF
    `sole operative trustee`
    `operative trustee with others`
    `custodian trustee`
    `custodian trustee with others`

GIVEN `the role` IS A TrusteeRole
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Corporation may accept the trust` IF
    CONSIDER `the role`
    WHEN `sole operative trustee` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `operative trustee with others` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `custodian trustee` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `custodian trustee with others` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 5: "Notwithstanding the trusts declared in section 4" all property vested in the Corporation "shall subject to the terms of the
-- instrument of trust (if any) affecting the same be nevertheless deemed to be so vested that in favour of any purchaser from and any
-- person dealing with the Corporation the Corporation shall have full power to sell, convey, assign, surrender, yield up, mortgage,
-- demise, re-assign, transfer or otherwise dispose of such property as fully and effectually as if the Corporation were the true and
-- beneficial owner thereof".
--
-- A buyer need not look behind the s 4 trusts or any implied trust. The one thing that cuts the protection down is the terms of a
-- written "instrument of trust" affecting the property. The section's heading says "Purchaser from Corporation not affected by
-- implied trusts".
GIVEN `the person is a purchaser or dealing with the Corporation` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `an instrument of trust affecting the property forbids the disposition` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Corporation disposes as if beneficial owner` IF
    `the person is a purchaser or dealing with the Corporation` AND (NOT `an instrument of trust affecting the property forbids the disposition`)

§§ `6 -- executing a deed under the corporate seal`

-- s 6(1): instruments requiring the seal "shall be sealed with the seal of the Corporation in the presence of the District Grand
-- Master of the Eastern Archipelago for the time being or his attorney duly authorised by a power of attorney deposited under section
-- 48 of the Conveyancing and Law of Property Act 1886 and shall also be signed by the said District Grand Master for the time being or
-- his attorney so authorised". (2): "Such signing shall be and be taken as sufficient evidence that the said seal was duly and properly
-- affixed and that the same is the lawful seal of the Corporation."
--
-- Two steps, both required: sealing IN THE PRESENCE of the right person, and SIGNING by the right person. An attorney counts only if
-- the power of attorney was DEPOSITED. The text does not require the witness to sealing and the signer to be the same person.
DECLARE Person IS ONE OF
    `the District Grand Master for the time being`
    `an attorney under a deposited power of attorney`
    `an attorney under an undeposited power of attorney`
    `a former District Grand Master`
    `a Lodge officer`
    `nobody`

GIVEN `who` IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `may attest for the Corporation` IF
    CONSIDER `who`
    WHEN `the District Grand Master for the time being` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an attorney under a deposited power of attorney` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `sealed with the Corporation's seal` IS A BOOLEAN, `sealed in the presence of` IS A Person, `signed by` IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the instrument is duly executed` IF
        `sealed with the Corporation's seal`
    AND `may attest for the Corporation` `sealed in the presence of`
    AND `may attest for the Corporation` `signed by`

GIVEN `signed by` IS A Person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the signature is sufficient evidence of due sealing` IF `may attest for the Corporation` `signed by`

§§ `8 -- rights of the Government`

-- s 8: "Nothing in this Ordinance shall affect the rights of the Government." [7/97]
-- What those rights are is not stated; s 5's purchaser protection and s 4's trusts cannot be read to cut them down.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Ordinance affects the rights of the Government` IS FALSE
